lder-13","page_id":"/docs/examples/watch-folder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/examples/watch-folder#see-also","content":"simple — single-shot extraction"},"649":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"page","content":"Chunking & Token Budgets","breadcrumbs":["Struktur","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking"},"650":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-0","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking","content":"How large documents are split and merged to fit within context windows."},"651":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-1","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#the-context-window-problem","content":"The context window problem"},"652":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-2","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"How splitting works"},"653":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-3","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#images-and-maximages","content":"Images and `maxImages`"},"654":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-4","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#why-simple-does-not-chunk","content":"Why `simple` does not chunk"},"655":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-5","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#merging-partial-results","content":"Merging partial results"},"656":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-6","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#llm-merge-parallel-doublepass","content":"LLM merge (parallel, doublePass)"},"657":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-7","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"Schema-aware auto-merge"},"658":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-8","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"Deduplication"},"659":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-9","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"When dedup matters"},"660":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-10","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"See also"},"661":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-11","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#the-context-window-problem","content":"LLMs have finite context windows. Documents — especially multi-page PDFs, large datasets, or many files at once — often exceed them. Struktur's chunker splits artifact contents into batches that fit within a configurable token budget (`chunkSize`)."},"662":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-12","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"Splitting happens at two levels:"},"663":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-13","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"**ArtifactSplitter:** splits a single large artifact's contents into smaller parts, respecting content slice boundaries (e.g., page boundaries)."},"664":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-14","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"**ArtifactBatcher:** groups artifacts or artifact parts into batches that stay within the token budget and optional image count limit."},"665":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-15","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#how-splitting-works","content":"The tokenizer uses a character-approximation (not exact token counting). `chunkSize` defaults to 10,000 tokens."},"666":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-16","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#images-and-maximages","content":"Some strategies accept `maxImages` to cap how many images appear per chunk. This matters when images consume disproportionate context. If a batch would exceed `maxImages`, extra images are moved to the next batch."},"667":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-17","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#why-simple-does-not-chunk","content":"The `simple` strategy loads all artifacts as-is. If the input exceeds the context window, the LLM call may fail or produce degraded results. For large inputs, use a chunked strategy."},"668":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-18","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#why-simple-does-not-chunk","content":"This is a deliberate trade-off: `simple` is fast and cheap for small inputs, not suitable for large ones."},"669":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-19","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#merging-partial-results","content":"When a document is split into N chunks and each chunk is extracted independently, you get N partial objects."},"670":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-20","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#merging-partial-results","content":"For a scalar-heavy schema (title, author, date), you want the best answer from all chunks."},"671":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-21","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#merging-partial-results","content":"For an array-heavy schema (line items, product listings), you want to concatenate all arrays and remove duplicates."},"672":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-22","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#llm-merge-parallel-doublepass","content":"These strategies send all partial results to a **merge model** in a single call, with a prompt asking it to produce a single coherent output. The merge model sees the full schema and all partial outputs."},"673":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-23","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#llm-merge-parallel-doublepass","content":"This is powerful but costs extra tokens."},"674":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-24","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"`parallelAutoMerge`, `sequentialAutoMerge`, and `doublePassAutoMerge` use `SmartDataMerger`:"},"675":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-25","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"**Arrays:** concatenated. `items` from chunk 1 + `items` from chunk 2 = `items` in merged."},"676":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-26","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"**Objects:** shallow-merged. Keys from later chunks overwrite keys from earlier ones."},"677":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-27","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"**Scalars:** prefer newer non-empty values."},"678":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-28","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#schema-aware-auto-merge","content":"This approach avoids an extra LLM call and works well for list-extraction schemas. It does not handle complex cross-chunk synthesis — for that, use LLM merge."},"679":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-29","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"After auto-merging concatenated arrays, there may be duplicates. Dedup runs in two stages:"},"680":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-30","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"**Stage 1: CRC32 hash-based.** Exact duplicates (byte-for-byte identical after stable JSON stringification) are removed without any LLM call. Fast and cheap."},"681":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-31","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"**Stage 2: LLM-based semantic dedup.** A dedupe model is given the merged array and asked to identify semantically equivalent entries (e.g., \"iPhone 15\" vs \"Apple iPhone 15 128GB\"). It returns a list of dot-path keys to remove (e.g., `items.3`)."},"682":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-32","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#deduplication","content":"Only the auto-merge variants include this step."},"683":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-33","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"Dedup is valuable when:"},"684":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-34","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"The same item legitimately appears in multiple chunks."},"685":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-35","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"The same document segment appears in multiple artifacts."},"686":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-36","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#when-dedup-matters","content":"Dedup adds token cost and latency. For schemas without arrays, or for inputs with no expected overlap, use strategies without auto-merge."},"687":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-37","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"The Extraction Pipeline — the full flow"},"688":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-38","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"Strategies — which strategies use chunking"},"689":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-39","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"parallel — chunked parallel extraction"},"690":{"id":"/docs/explanation/chunking-40","page_id":"/docs/explanation/chunking","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation/chunking#see-also","content":"sequential — chunked sequential extraction"},"691":{"id":"/docs/explanation","page_id":"/docs/explanation","type":"page","content":"Explanation","breadcrumbs":["Struktur","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation"},"692":{"id":"/docs/explanation-0","page_id":"/docs/explanation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/explanation#topics","content":"Topics"},"693":{"id":"/docs/explanation-1","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation","content":"Deep dives into Struktur's architecture, strategies, and design decisions."},"694":{"id":"/docs/explanation-2","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation#topics","content":"pipeline — How data flows through Struktur"},"695":{"id":"/docs/explanation-3","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/explanation#topics","content":"chunking — Token-aware document splitting"},"696":{"id":"/docs/explanation-4","page_id":"/docs/explanation","tags